1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:00,664
In 1965,

2
00:00:00,664 --> 00:00:06,126
Gordon Moore predicted that chips would double their number of components every year.

3
00:00:06,617 --> 00:00:10,241
In 1975, he changed it to about every two years.

4
00:00:10,241 --> 00:00:13,110
And for decades, the industry kept up.

5
00:00:13,617 --> 00:00:15,580
So what if it never slowed down?

6
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:17,851
Clock speeds would still be climbing.

7
00:00:17,851 --> 00:00:21,778
Instead, they stalled at around four gigahertz in the mid-2000s.

8
00:00:22,283 --> 00:00:23,398
The reason is heat.

9
00:00:23,398 --> 00:00:26,038
Air-cooled chips hit roughly a hundred watts.

10
00:00:26,038 --> 00:00:28,854
Push them much harder, and they cook themselves.

11
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:30,902
And there's a harder wall.

12
00:00:30,902 --> 00:00:34,186
Silicon atoms sit about a quarter of a nanometer apart.

13
00:00:34,186 --> 00:00:36,932
You can't build a switch smaller than an atom.

14
00:00:37,417 --> 00:00:41,940
So chips are starting to grow upward instead, stacking layers like a skyscraper.

15
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:44,224
What would it look like inside one?

16
00:00:44,224 --> 00:00:45,542
Full story on the channel.
